---
/**
 * Math toolkit (2026-11-05): short refreshers for the math tools lessons use, so a reader who has
 * not touched logarithms since school can catch up in a minute, in place, and go back. Built from
 * the math empathy audit (docs/expansion/audits/empathy_*.md). Equation Cards link here by anchor.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Math toolkit — Biochemistrypedia" description="Short, friendly refreshers on the math tools biology lessons use: e and ln, powers and roots, log scales, unit prefixes, Greek letters and subscripts, and how to read an equation.">
  <article class="wrap prose-col mt" style="padding-top: var(--ddp-space-12)">
    <p class="eyebrow">Math toolkit</p>
    <h1>The math tools, one minute each</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Biology uses a small set of math tools again and again. If a lesson sends you here, read the
      one section you need, then go back. Nobody does these by hand: a calculator or phone does the
      arithmetic. What matters is knowing what the tool means and roughly what answer to expect.
    </p>

    <nav class="mt-nav" aria-label="Sections">
      <a href="#read">Reading an equation</a>
      <a href="#subscripts">Subscripts, superscripts, Greek letters</a>
      <a href="#units">Units and prefixes</a>
      <a href="#powers">Powers and roots</a>
      <a href="#e-and-ln">e and ln</a>
      <a href="#log-scales">Log scales</a>
      <a href="#ratios">Rates, ratios and "per"</a>
    </nav>

    <section id="read">
      <h2>Reading an equation</h2>
      <p>An equation is a sentence. Read it in words before you read it in symbols.</p>
      <p class="mt-eq">current = conductance × driving force</p>
      <p>
        That says: the more open the channels, and the harder the ions are pushed, the bigger the
        current. Three habits help with every equation:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Ask what it is for.</strong> What question does it answer? (How much current? How fast? How many?)</li>
        <li><strong>Name every symbol out loud.</strong> If you cannot say a symbol, you cannot hold it in your head. The next section shows how to say them.</li>
        <li><strong>Try an extreme.</strong> Set one quantity to zero, or make it huge, and see if the answer makes sense. If the channels are all closed (conductance zero), the current should be zero, and it is.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="subscripts">
      <h2>Subscripts, superscripts and Greek letters</h2>
      <p>
        A small lowered label is a <strong>subscript</strong>. It says <em>which</em> one: E<sub>K</sub> is
        “E sub K”, the equilibrium potential <em>of potassium</em>. g<sub>Na</sub> is the conductance of sodium.
        A subscript is a name tag, not a number to multiply.
      </p>
      <p>
        A small raised number is a <strong>superscript</strong>, usually a power: 10<sup>3</sup> is
        “ten to the three”, 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000. In genetics a superscript can also be part of a
        name: I<sup>A</sup> is the A allele of the I gene.
      </p>
      <p>Greek letters you will meet, and how to say them:</p>
      <dl class="mt-greek">
        <div><dt>Δ</dt><dd>“delta”: a change, as in ΔV, the change in voltage</dd></div>
        <div><dt>τ</dt><dd>“tau”: a time constant, how long something takes to change most of the way</dd></div>
        <div><dt>λ</dt><dd>“lambda”: a length constant in a nerve, or a growth multiplier in ecology (lessons say which)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>π</dt><dd>“pi”: 3.14…, or, in physiology, osmotic pressure (π<sub>c</sub>)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Ψ</dt><dd>“psi”: water potential in plants</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Σ</dt><dd>“sigma”: add up all of these</dd></div>
        <div><dt>μ</dt><dd>“mu”: the prefix micro (one millionth), as in μm or μM</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section id="units">
      <h2>Units and prefixes</h2>
      <p>Prefixes are just powers of ten. Each step down the list is a thousand times smaller.</p>
      <table class="mt-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Means</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>kilo (k)</td><td>× 1,000</td><td>1 kg = 1,000 g</td></tr>
          <tr><td>milli (m)</td><td>÷ 1,000</td><td>1 mV = 0.001 V</td></tr>
          <tr><td>micro (μ)</td><td>÷ 1,000,000</td><td>1 μm = 0.001 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>nano (n)</td><td>÷ 10<sup>9</sup></td><td>1 nM = 0.001 μM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>pico (p)</td><td>÷ 10<sup>12</sup></td><td>1 pA = 0.001 nA</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Units multiply and cancel like numbers. A conductance in millisiemens times a voltage in
        millivolts gives a current: mS × mV = μA, because milli × milli is micro (a thousandth of a
        thousandth). If the units of your answer come out wrong, the calculation is wrong: units are a
        free check, not a test.
      </p>
      <p>“Per” means divide: 5 L per min is 5 L ÷ 1 min.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="powers">
      <h2>Powers and roots</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>x<sup>2</sup> is x × x. x<sup>3</sup> is x × x × x.</li>
        <li>A negative power means one over: 10<sup>−3</sup> = 1/1,000 = 0.001.</li>
        <li>A fractional power is a root: x<sup>1/2</sup> = √x, and x<sup>1/3</sup> is the cube root.</li>
        <li>Other fractions sit in between. M<sup>0.75</sup> grows more slowly than M: double M and M<sup>0.75</sup> goes up about 1.7 times, not 2.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        On a calculator or phone, use the <kbd>^</kbd> or <kbd>x<sup>y</sup></kbd> key: 100 ^ 0.75 = 31.6.
        To sanity-check, ask which whole powers it sits between: 100<sup>0.5</sup> = 10 and 100<sup>1</sup> = 100,
        so 100<sup>0.75</sup> must land in between. It does.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="e-and-ln">
      <h2>e and ln</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>e</strong> is a number, about 2.718. It appears whenever something changes at a rate
        proportional to how much of it there is: a cell population growing, a charge leaking away, a
        drug being cleared. You never need its digits; you need three landmarks for decay:
      </p>
      <table class="mt-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Value</th><th>In words</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>e<sup>−1</sup></td><td>0.37</td><td>after one time constant, about 37% is left</td></tr>
          <tr><td>e<sup>−2</sup></td><td>0.14</td><td>after two, about 14%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>e<sup>−3</sup></td><td>0.05</td><td>after three, about 5%: nearly done</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        So e<sup>−t/τ</sup> means “what fraction is left after time t, if the time constant is τ”.
        Rising curves use 1 − e<sup>−t/τ</sup>: after one time constant a charging membrane is
        about 63% of the way (1 − 0.37).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>ln</strong> (the natural log) undoes e. If e<sup>x</sup> = 5, then x = ln 5 = 1.61.
        It answers “e to what power gives this?”. Two landmarks: ln 1 = 0, and ln of a number below 1
        is negative (ln 0.5 = −0.69). <strong>log</strong> without a letter usually means base 10: log 1,000 = 3,
        because 10<sup>3</sup> = 1,000. Use the <kbd>ln</kbd> and <kbd>log</kbd> keys on a calculator.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="log-scales">
      <h2>Log scales</h2>
      <p>
        On an ordinary axis, equal steps are equal <em>additions</em>: 10, 20, 30. On a log axis, equal
        steps are equal <em>multiplications</em>: 1, 10, 100, 1,000. A log axis lets a mouse and a whale,
        or a survivor count falling from 1,000 to 1, fit on one graph.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two shapes to recognise: something that shrinks by the same fraction each step is a straight
        line on a log axis; and a power law (y = a x<sup>b</sup>) is a straight line on a log–log plot,
        with slope b.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ratios">
      <h2>Rates, ratios and “per”</h2>
      <p>
        Many biology equations are bookkeeping: what comes in, what goes out, per unit of time. Before
        trusting a formula, try one round number: if blood flow is 5 L per minute and each litre gives
        up 50 mL of oxygen, the body takes 5 × 50 = 250 mL of oxygen per minute. Every “per” is a
        division you can say out loud.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="mt-back"><a href="javascript:history.back()">← Back to the lesson</a></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .mt .lede { font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--ddp-color-muted); }
  .mt-nav { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.45rem; margin: 1.2rem 0 1.6rem; }
  .mt-nav a { font: 500 0.8rem var(--ddp-font-mono); padding: 0.3em 0.75em; border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); border-radius: 999px; text-decoration: none; color: var(--ddp-color-primary); }
  .mt section { margin: 2.2rem 0; }
  .mt-eq { text-align: center; font-family: var(--ddp-font-serif, Georgia, serif); font-size: 1.25rem; color: var(--ddp-color-primary); }
  .mt-greek { display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0.6rem 0; }
  .mt-greek div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2.4rem 1fr; gap: 0.6rem; align-items: baseline; }
  .mt-greek dt { font-family: var(--ddp-font-serif, Georgia, serif); font-size: 1.4rem; color: var(--ddp-color-primary); text-align: center; }
  .mt-greek dd { margin: 0; }
  .mt-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0.8rem 0 1rem; font-size: 0.95em; }
  .mt-table th { text-align: left; font: 500 0.75rem var(--ddp-font-mono); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--ddp-color-muted); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ddp-color-line); padding: 0.4em 0.6em; }
  .mt-table td { padding: 0.5em 0.6em; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); }
  .mt kbd { font: 500 0.85em var(--ddp-font-mono); border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); border-radius: 5px; padding: 0 0.35em; }
  .mt sub, .mt sup { font-size: 0.72em; line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; }
  .mt sub { bottom: -0.28em; } .mt sup { top: -0.5em; }
  .mt-back { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
</style>
